1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:05,150
An exchange requirement defines the information that one exchange must carry.

2
00:00:06,050 --> 00:00:13,131
In this process, the information is exchanged as forms: an inspection form, a log entry, a triage record,

3
00:00:13,131 --> 00:00:15,289
an assignment notice, and so on.

4
00:00:15,289 --> 00:00:18,189
Each form becomes one exchange requirement.

5
00:00:19,089 --> 00:00:21,398
Here is the defect inspection form.

6
00:00:21,398 --> 00:00:28,061
Each field will become an information unit: inspection number, date, inspector, location with storey,

7
00:00:28,061 --> 00:00:29,777
space and grid, and so on.

8
00:00:30,677 --> 00:00:34,320
Double-click the data object Defect inspection form.

9
00:00:34,320 --> 00:00:38,314
Choose Create New ER, keep the name, and click Create ER.

10
00:00:39,877 --> 00:00:42,822
The new exchange requirement opens on the right.

11
00:00:42,822 --> 00:00:45,338
Click plus IU to add an information unit.

12
00:00:46,238 --> 00:00:47,950
Name it Inspection number.

13
00:00:47,950 --> 00:00:53,022
Keep String or Text as the data type, tick Mandatory, and write a definition.

14
00:00:54,988 --> 00:00:57,629
Add two more units, Location and Storey.

15
00:00:57,629 --> 00:01:03,308
Then select Storey and click the indent button: Storey becomes a sub-unit of Location.

16
00:01:04,208 --> 00:01:09,065
Synonyms, examples and figures can be added in the same editor.

17
00:01:09,065 --> 00:01:17,392
Repeating this for all eight forms takes time, so open the catch-up file DMBCF_5d_exchange_requirements.idm.

18
00:01:18,292 --> 00:01:26,428
In the catch-up file, a root exchange requirement, Defect management information, groups eight sub-ERs, one per form.

19
00:01:26,428 --> 00:01:29,001
Each data object is linked to its ER.

20
00:01:29,901 --> 00:01:37,152
Select the defect inspection form: ten information units, with data types, mandatory flags and definitions.

21
00:01:37,152 --> 00:01:39,117
Location has three sub-units.

